    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **36 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/TelephoneE164.java`:\n\nAdded a `hasValidNationalLength` check in `normalize(...)`, applied after the existing 8\u201315 digit E.164 range check:\n- `+61` (digits start with `61`) \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits\n- `+1` (digits start with `1`) \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits\n- other country codes \u2192 unconstrained beyond the overall 8\u201315 range\n\nA wrong length makes `normalize` return `null`, which `ValidateOwnerFields` already turns into an `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 **400** with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`. I also updated the class javadoc to document the rule.\n\n**Why here:** `TelephoneE164.normalize` is the single source of E.164 truth used by both `ValidateOwnerFields` (400 on invalid) and `EnsureUniqueTelephone`, so the rule stays consistent across the pipeline.\n\n**Verification:** The new `Cp26Tests` (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the pre-existing telephone tests remain green \u2014 `Cp08` (`0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` with 9 national digits; `+64 21 123 456` kept verbatim, unconstrained), `Cp02`, and `Cp03` (national/international `+61` forms still collide as duplicates). The default `uniqueTelephone()` fixture (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`) stays valid, so no other checkpoint broke.",
